{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat task.md; cat history.md'","aggregated_output":"# Google QUEST Q&A Labeling\n\nLink: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge  (overview, data and evaluation tabs)\nMetric direction: higher is better\n\n## Official description (overview + evaluation + data)\n(source: MLE-bench `description.md`, github.com/openai/mle-bench)\n\n# Overview\n## Description\nComputers are really good at answering questions with single, verifiable answers. But, humans are often still better at answering questions about opinions, recommendations, or personal experiences.\n\nHumans are better at addressing subjective questions that require a deeper, multidimensional understanding of context - something computers aren't trained to do well…yet.. Questions can take many forms - some have multi-sentence elaborations, others may be simple curiosity or a fully developed problem. They can have multiple intents, or seek advice and opinions. Some may be helpful and others interesting. Some are simple right or wrong.\n\n![Human computable dimensions](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/google-research/human_computable_dimensions_1.png)\n\nUnfortunately, it’s hard to build better subjective question-answering algorithms because of a lack of data and predictive models. That’s why the [CrowdSource](https://crowdsource.google.com/csf/#/) team at Google Research, a group dedicated to advancing NLP and other types of ML science via crowdsourcing, has collected data on a number of these quality scoring aspects.\n\nIn this competition, you’re challenged to use this new dataset to build predictive algorithms for different subjective aspects of question-answering. The question-answer pairs were gathered from nearly 70 different websites, in a \"common-sense\" fashion. Our raters received minimal guidance and training, and relied largely on their subjective interpretation of the prompts. As such, each prompt was crafted in the most intuitive fashion so that raters could simply use their common-sense to complete the task. By lessening our dependency on complicated and opaque rating guidelines, we hope to increase the re-use value of this data set. What you see is what you get!\n\nDemonstrating these subjective labels can be predicted reliably can shine a new light on this research area. Results from this competition will inform the way future intelligent Q&A systems will get built, hopefully contributing to them becoming more human-like.\n\n## Evaluation\n\nSubmissions are evaluated on the mean column-wise [Spearman's correlation coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spearman%27s_rank_correlation_coefficient). The Spearman's rank correlation is computed for each target column, and the mean of these values is calculated for the submission score.\n\nFor each qa_id in the test set, you must predict a probability for each target variable. The predictions should be in the range [0,1]. The file should contain a header and have the following format:\n\n```\nqa_id,question_asker_intent_understanding,...,answer_well_written\n6,0.0,...,0.5\n8,0.5,...,0.1\n18,1.0,...,0.0\netc.\n```\n\n## Timeline\n\n- February 3, 2020 - Team Merger deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.\n- February 3, 2020 - Entry deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.\n- February 10, 2020 - Final submission deadline.\n\nAll deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.\n\n## Prizes\n\n- 1st place - $10,000\n- 2nd place - $7,000\n- 3rd place - $5,000\n- 4th place - $2,000\n- 5th place - $1,000\n\n## Notebooks Requirements\n\nSubmissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. Your Notebook will re-run automatically against an unseen test set, and needs to output a file named submission.csv. You can still train a model offline, upload it as a dataset, and use the notebook exclusively to perform inference.\n\nIn order for the \"Submit to Competition\" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:\n\n  - CPU Notebooks <= 9 hours run-time\n  - GPU Notebooks <= 2 hours run-time\n  - Internet must be turned off\n\nIn synchronous Notebooks-only competitions, note that a submission that results in an error--either within the notebook or within the process of scoring--will count against your daily submission limit and will not return the specific error message. This is to limit probing the private test set.\n\nPlease note - committing your Notebook will result in a full execution of your code from top-to-bottom.\n\nPlease see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#kernels-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit.\n\n## Citation\n\nDanicky, Praveen Paritosh, Walter Reade, Addison Howard, Mark McDonald. (2019). Google QUEST Q&A Labeling. Kaggle. https://kaggle.com/competitions/google-quest-challenge\n\n# Dataset Description\n\nThe data for this competition includes questions and answers from various StackExchange properties. Your task is to predict target values of 30 labels for each question-answer pair.\n\nThe list of 30 target labels are the same as the column names in the `sample_submission.csv` file. Target labels with the prefix `question_` relate to the `question_title` and/or `question_body` features in the data. Target labels with the prefix `answer_` relate to the `answer` feature.\n\nEach row contains a single question and a single answer to that question, along with additional features. The training data contains rows with some duplicated questions (but with different answers). The test data does not contain any duplicated questions.\n\nThis is not a binary prediction challenge. Target labels are aggregated from multiple raters, and can have continuous values in the range `[0,1]`. Therefore, predictions must also be in that range.\n\nSince this is a synchronous re-run competition, you only have access to the Public test set. For planning purposes, the re-run test set is no larger than 10,000 rows, and less than 8 Mb uncompressed.\n\nAdditional information about the labels and collection method will be provided by the competition sponsor in the forum.\n\n## File descriptions\n\n  - **train.csv** - the training data (target labels are the last 30 columns)\n  - **test.csv** - the test set (you must predict 30 labels for each test set row)\n  - **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format; column names are the 30 target labels\n\n\n## Summary from our notes\nGoogle QUEST Q&A Labeling (Kaggle, 2019-20). For 6,079 StackExchange question-answer pairs (question_title, question_body, answer, plus category/host), predict 30 continuous quality labels (e.g. question_asker_intent _understanding, answer_helpful) that are averages of crowd ratings and take a few discrete values. Metric: mean Spearman correlation over the 30 columns, higher is better. Solutions fine-tune BERT-family models on question and answer text, and post-process predictions (rounding to the discrete levels) because Spearman depends on ranks.\n# Current version and earlier versions\n\nCurrent version: **v46**, leaderboard score 0.38762 (higher is better).\n\n## Earlier versions (oldest first; goal of the change, summary of the change, score the version reached)\n...(older versions omitted)\nreprocessing to evaluate that variant. | change: Disabled the NER detector (ner_model set to None), turned off process_math (True -> False), and changed experiment_dir from '2020_01_20__20_10_11__640' to '2020_01_21__07_28_36__552' to load a different set of saved fold models. | score after: 0.38163 (-0.00657)\nv28 -> v29: Enable processing of mathematical expressions in the text preprocessing step at inference time to slightly improve prediction quality. | change: Changed the boolean flag process_math from False to True to turn on math-specific preprocessing. | score after: 0.38186 (+0.00023)\nv29 -> v30: Enable the pretrained NER model at inference and point the evaluator to a different experiment directory so fold checkpoints from that experiment are used (to improve final submission score). | change: Uncommented the NERDetector.from_model_dir call (enabled NER model instantiation) and commented-out the previous ner_model = None line; changed experiment_dir path to a different experiment folder (switched which saved fold checkpoints will be loaded | score after: 0.38503 (+0.00317)\nv30 -> v31: Replace the inline BiEncoderEvaluator definition with a shared implementation and point the notebook at a different experiment directory (use a different trained run) for inference. | change: Removed an in-notebook BiEncoderEvaluator subclass (and its helper methods) and instead import BiEncoderEvaluator from kaggle_google_qa_labeling.evaluator.BiEncoderEvaluator; updated experiment_dir to a different run id. | score after: 0.38356 (-0.00147)\nv31 -> v32: No code changes — likely a metadata/version bump or re-run; nothing to modify in the pipeline. | change: No changes to code (empty diff). File is identical to previous version. | score after: n/a\nv32 -> v33: Use rank-based blending instead of mean averaging to combine fold predictions (and add minor notebook inspection outputs). | change: Changed BiEncoderEvaluator parameter blend_strategy from 'mean' to 'rank' to switch blending to rank-averaging; replaced final cell display with a sorted column view and added a test_df sample lookup for quick inspection. | score after: 0.38345\nv33 -> v34: Point inference to a different experiment checkpoint and change the fold blending strategy (rank → mean) to adjust ensemble behavior; also add small inspection commands and change which output column/index is being examined. | change: Updated experiment_dir literal to a different run, changed BiEncoderEvaluator blend_strategy from 'rank' to 'mean', added a '!ls' listing command, and modified a couple of DataFrame inspection lines (different output column and test index). | score after: 0.37705 (-0.00639)\nv34 -> v35: Disable the NER-based processing and change experiment directory / processing flag to use a different inference configuration (likely to speed up or improve predictions). | change: Commented out loading of the NERDetector and set ner_model = None; changed process_math from True to False; updated experiment_dir to a different run id; removed an ls call and adjusted a display line (out_df). | score after: 0.38061 (+0.00356)\nv35 -> v36: Try a cross-encoder evaluation pipeline instead of the previous bi-encoder inference (update experiment path and visualize prediction distribution). | change: Updated experiment_dir path; replaced the BiEncoderEvaluator instantiation with a CrossEncoderEvaluator (same args: device, bs, blend_strategy, models_dir_name, ner_model, process_math) and commented the old bi-encoder lines; final cell now plots a h | score after: 0.37590 (-0.00471)\nv36 -> v37: Introduce NER-aware text cropping and switch to a bi-encoder evaluation path (with tokenization and blending) to try improving inference predictions. | change: Added CrossEncoderEvaluator and BiEncoderEvaluator classes that load tokenizers, perform NER-aware cropping (get_tqa_codes/get_crops), build Cross/Bi datasets and dataloaders, and evaluate with postprocessing (blend_sigmoids + reordering). Enabled a  | score after: n/a\nv37 -> v38: Prevent overwriting columns of predictions with degenerate/constant adjusted values during post-processing so submission keeps meaningful original predictions where appropriate. | change: Introduce y_pred_new = y_pred.copy() and only replace a column with the post-processed values if the adjusted column isn't constant (len(set(b_)) != 1). Use y_pred_new for the submission output; remove a debug cell and add a small comment/debug line. | score after: 0.42704\nv38 -> v39: Remove / comment out old CrossEncoder/BiEncoder evaluator implementations (and the tokenizer-based cropping path) as part of a refactor; adjust configuration/path literals. | change: Large-scale commenting-out of CrossEncoderEvaluator and BiEncoderEvaluator class implementations (tokenizer/NER-based crop & data loader logic), plus multiple literal/config value changes elsewhere (many constants/paths updated). | score after: 0.42442 (-0.00262)\nv39 -> v40: Load a different saved bi-encoder experiment and remove the custom per-column blending/postprocessing to test raw ensemble predictions (simplify pipeline / try a different checkpoint). | change: Reorganized imports to use new module paths, changed experiment_dir to a different run ID, and commented out the custom coeffs dict and the loop that performed per-column quantized blending — disabling that postprocessing. Minor cleanup of shell ls a | score after: 0.38139 (-0.04303)\nv40 -> v41: Introduce cross-encoder inference and NER-/math-aware crop tokenization, blend its outputs with existing bi-encoder predictions, apply binning/post-processing, and produce an improved submission. | change: Replaced input experiment path; added many imports and dataset utilities; implemented/expanded CrossEncoderEvaluator (model loading, _get_data); added crop-based tokenization and NER/math preprocessing utilities; wired CrossDataset/CrossDataset loade | score after: n/a\nv41 -> v42: Fix an edge case in discretization where a column becomes constant after KBinsDiscretizer by reverting that column to the original continuous predictions to preserve variance. | change: After applying KBinsDiscretizer, iterate columns and if a column collapsed to a single bin value, replace that column in y_pred_new with the original continuous predictions y_pred for that column. | score after: n/a\nv42 -> v43: Use a variance-based test instead of exact-unique check to detect collapsed